      "annotations": {
        "change_summary": "Replaced the initial-level-set passthrough with a drillhole-aware height-field solver: detect that labels are ordered above/below contacts, fit a Gaussian RBF + affine contact surface, then optimise its coefficients against the published objective (logistic labels, contact mean/variance, sketch when present) under a wall-time bound. Generic RBF correction of the initial field remains as a fallback for non-height-field, no-contact, and 2-D sketch fixtures.",
        "parent_sha256": "3d1f8f7eda036b454b01466f33520daad3c565bcc42da35799840863a818c44f",
        "rationale": "Measured baseline returned the initial level set (loss_total 405.08, 54 sign mismatches, contact RMSE 5.56). Inspection showed all 70 drillhole labels lie on four vertical holes and are separated by exactly four 0.5 contacts, so a smooth single-valued contact surface (interior above) is the natural geometry. A Gaussian RBF + affine surface interpolates the contacts exactly; choosing the kernel width and a small 1-D vertical shift, then bounded Nelder-Mead refinement, reduced the public objective to 6.637 (0 label mismatches, contact RMSE 0.264, about 26 s of the 120 s budget). This is close to the analytic floor from four contact-logistic terms plus the nearest labelled cells. Public selfcheck and synthetic 2-D sketch / scattered-3-D fixtures also return finite fields with both signs."
      },
